\title{A Rigorous Foundation for the Global Label Min-Cut Problem}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-27}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete, self-contained mathematical foundation for the \textbf{Global Label Min-Cut (GLMC)} problem on finite labeled graphs. In GLMC, every edge of an undirected graph carries a label from a finite palette of size $p$, and the cost of a bipartition of the vertex set is the number of \emph{distinct labels} appearing on the edges that cross it; the objective is to minimize this cost over all nontrivial bipartitions. We formalize the model precisely---vertices, labeled edges, crossing edges, cut-label sets, the cut value, the set of proper cuts, and the global optimum---and establish its core theory: the objective is bounded above by the palette size $p$; a proper cut exists whenever the graph has at least two vertices; the optimum is genuinely attained by a witnessing proper cut and is a true minimum (both the lower-bound and attainment directions are proved); and the optimum is zero exactly when the graph already admits an edge-free bipartition. The optimum is realized as an explicit finite (exponential-time) brute-force solver over the $2^{|V|}$ vertex subsets, and our correctness theorems certify that this solver returns the genuine minimum. We additionally document, with proof-level care, why a previously conjectured ``polynomial-in-$n$-and-$p$, fixed-genus'' algorithm cannot be obtained by the proposed treewidth pipeline: the running time the pipeline yields is quasi-polynomial rather than polynomial, the underlying genus-to-treewidth bound is false without an additive $+1$ correction, and an NP-hardness backdrop makes the polynomial target implausible. The contribution is a trustworthy, foundation-independent kernel on which sharper algorithmic results can be built.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Classical minimum-cut theory measures the cost of separating a graph by counting \emph{edges} (or summing edge weights). In many applications, however, edges are grouped into \emph{types}, and the meaningful unit of cost is the type, not the individual edge: disabling one type disables all edges bearing it simultaneously. Communication conduits, shared infrastructure corridors, regulatory-molecule influences, and vulnerability classes in security systems all share this structure. The resulting optimization---minimize the number of distinct edge-types whose removal disconnects the graph---is the \textbf{Global Label Min-Cut (GLMC)} problem. The ``global'' qualifier indicates that we seek \emph{any} nontrivial bipartition, not a separation of two designated terminals.

This paper provides the foundational layer for GLMC: an exact model and the theorems that make the objective well posed and computable. We separate the \emph{mathematical content} of the problem---what it asks and what is provably true about it, which we establish in full---from the \emph{runtime conjecture} originally attached to it, which we analyze and show cannot be obtained as stated.

Throughout, we fix a finite vertex type $V$ and a finite label type $L$, with $n = |V|$ and $p = |L|$, both equipped with decidable equality so that all set operations below are finite computations.

\subsection{Related problems and context}

GLMC belongs to the family of \emph{labeled} (colored) graph optimization problems, in which an edge coloring modulates the cost of a structure. Its closest relatives are \emph{Minimum Label Cut} (separate two designated terminals $s,t$ using the fewest edge-labels) and \emph{Minimum Label Spanning Tree} (connect the graph using the fewest labels). GLMC is the \emph{global, terminal-free} cut analogue: the cheapest nontrivial bipartition rather than the cheapest $s$--$t$ separation. Two features distinguish the labeled objective from classical weighted min-cut. First, the cost is the \emph{cardinality of a label set}, not a sum of weights, making it a set-cover-like quantity that often inherits NP-hardness. Second, removing a label is an \emph{all-or-nothing} action on every edge of that label, which destroys the local exchange arguments underlying classical max-flow/min-cut duality. A verified foundation is therefore valuable before any algorithmic claim: intuitions transported from weighted min-cut are unreliable here.

\subsection{Notation and conventions}

We write $A^c = V \setminus A$ for complement, $|S|$ for cardinality, and identify a finite ``set of edges'' with a finite collection of triples (parallel edges and repeated labels are permitted; only \emph{distinct labels} are counted). All quantities are natural numbers; no subtraction appears. The empty-minimum convention ($\min \varnothing = 0$) applies only to $\mathrm{glmcOpt}$ and only when $|V| \le 1$, where no proper cut exists.

\section{The model}

\subsection{Instances}

\begin{definition}[Instance]
An \emph{instance} of GLMC is a finite set of labeled edges $E \subseteq V \times V \times L$, where a triple $(u, v, \ell) \in E$ denotes an undirected edge $\{u, v\}$ carrying label $\ell$.
\end{definition}

The encoding is directed (ordered triples) but models an undirected problem faithfully, because every quantity below depends only on the unordered pair: storing $(u, v, \ell)$ versus $(v, u, \ell)$ changes neither whether the edge crosses a given cut nor its label.

\subsection{Cuts and crossing edges}

\begin{definition}[Cut, crossing]
A \emph{cut} is a subset $A \subseteq V$, inducing the bipartition $(A, A^c)$. An edge $(u, v, \ell)$ \emph{crosses} $A$ iff exactly one endpoint lies in $A$:
\[ (u \in A) \neq (v \in A). \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cut-label set, cut value]
The \emph{cut-label set} of $A$ is the set of labels on crossing edges,
\[ \mathrm{cutLabels}(E, A) = \bigl\{\, \ell : \exists\, (u,v,\ell) \in E,\ (u \in A) \neq (v \in A) \,\bigr\}, \]
realized as the image, under the label projection $(u,v,\ell) \mapsto \ell$, of the crossing edges of $E$. The \emph{cut value} is its cardinality,
\[ \mathrm{cutValue}(E, A) = \bigl|\mathrm{cutLabels}(E, A)\bigr|. \]
\end{definition}

The defining modeling choice is that $\mathrm{cutValue}$ counts \emph{distinct labels}, not crossing edges: parallel crossing edges of the same label contribute a single unit of cost.

\subsection{Proper cuts and the optimum}

\begin{definition}[Proper cuts]
The set of \emph{proper cuts} of $V$ is
\[ \mathrm{properCuts}(V) = \{\, A \subseteq V : A \neq \varnothing \text{ and } A \neq V \,\}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[GLMC optimum]
The \emph{GLMC optimum} is the minimum cut value over proper cuts, with value $0$ by convention when no proper cut exists:
\[ \mathrm{glmcOpt}(E) = \min_{A \in \mathrm{properCuts}(V)} \mathrm{cutValue}(E, A), \]
where the empty minimum is taken to be $0$.
\end{definition}

Because $V$ is finite, $\mathrm{properCuts}(V)$ is a finite collection of at most $2^{n}$ subsets, and $\mathrm{glmcOpt}$ is a finite computation. Operationally, $\mathrm{glmcOpt}$ \emph{is} the brute-force solver: enumerate proper cuts, evaluate $\mathrm{cutValue}$ on each, take the minimum.

\section{Boundedness and existence}

\begin{theorem}[Cut value bounded by palette size]
For every instance $E$ and every cut $A$, $\mathrm{cutValue}(E, A) \le p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\mathrm{cutLabels}(E, A)$ is a subset of the finite label type $L$, hence its cardinality is at most $|L| = p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of a proper cut]
If $n = |V| \ge 2$, then $\mathrm{properCuts}(V) \neq \varnothing$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose distinct $a \neq b$. Then $A = \{a\}$ is nonempty and $A \neq V$ since $b \notin A$, so $A \in \mathrm{properCuts}(V)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Membership characterization]
For $A \subseteq V$, $A \in \mathrm{properCuts}(V) \iff A \neq \varnothing \text{ and } A \neq V$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate by unfolding the definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Optimum bounded by palette size]
For every instance $E$, $\mathrm{glmcOpt}(E) \le p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If no proper cut exists, $\mathrm{glmcOpt}(E) = 0 \le p$. Otherwise every element of $\{\mathrm{cutValue}(E, A) : A \in \mathrm{properCuts}(V)\}$ is at most $p$, so its minimum is too.
\end{proof}

\section{Correctness: the optimum is the true minimum}

\begin{theorem}[Lower-bound correctness]
For every instance $E$ and every proper cut $A \in \mathrm{properCuts}(V)$, $\mathrm{glmcOpt}(E) \le \mathrm{cutValue}(E, A)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\mathrm{cutValue}(E, A)$ is one of the elements over which $\mathrm{glmcOpt}(E)$ minimizes; a minimum is $\le$ each element. When no such $A$ exists the statement is vacuous.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment]
If $n = |V| \ge 2$, then for every instance $E$ there exists a proper cut $A$ with $\mathrm{cutValue}(E, A) = \mathrm{glmcOpt}(E)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the existence theorem $\mathrm{properCuts}(V)$ is nonempty, so the finite, nonempty set of natural numbers $\{\mathrm{cutValue}(E, A)\}$ attains its minimum at some $A^\star$, and by definition $\mathrm{cutValue}(E, A^\star) = \mathrm{glmcOpt}(E)$.
\end{proof}

Together these state that $\mathrm{glmcOpt}(E)$ is a \emph{witnessed} minimum: it lower-bounds the cost of every split, and some explicit split achieves it.

\begin{theorem}[Zero optimum from a separated bipartition]
Let $A \in \mathrm{properCuts}(V)$ satisfy $\neg\bigl((u \in A) \neq (v \in A)\bigr)$ for all $(u,v,\ell) \in E$. Then $\mathrm{glmcOpt}(E) = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With no crossing edges, $\mathrm{cutLabels}(E, A) = \varnothing$, so $\mathrm{cutValue}(E, A) = 0$. By lower-bound correctness $\mathrm{glmcOpt}(E) \le 0$, hence $= 0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algo}[Exhaustive enumeration, BF]
Given an instance $E$ over $V$ with $|V| = n$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Enumerate every subset $A \subseteq V$ (there are $2^n$).
\item Discard $A = \varnothing$ and $A = V$.
\item For each remaining $A$, scan $E$, collect labels of crossing edges into a set, record its cardinality.
\item Return the minimum recorded value (or $0$ if none).
\end{enumerate}
\end{algo}

By the correctness theorems, BF returns $\mathrm{glmcOpt}(E)$. Its time is $O(2^n \cdot |E|)$ with $O(p)$ working space per cut. Sound pruning: early-exit on a value-$0$ cut (globally optimal by the disconnection theorem), and use the a priori certificate $\mathrm{glmcOpt}(E) \le p$.

\section{The runtime conjecture: why the proposed pipeline fails}

The originating motivation conjectured a deterministic algorithm solving GLMC on genus-$g$ surface-embedded graphs in time $2^{O(g)} \cdot n^{O(1)} \cdot p^{O(1)}$ via (1) a genus-to-treewidth bound, (2) a tree-decomposition dynamic program, (3) composition. This cannot be carried out as stated, for foundation-independent reasons.

\paragraph{Quasi-polynomial vs.\ polynomial.} A treewidth-$w$ dynamic program for a labeled cut objective is exponential in $w$. With $w = O(\sqrt{(g+1)\,n})$, composition yields $2^{O(\sqrt{(g+1)\,n})} \cdot p^{O(\sqrt{(g+1)\,n})} \cdot n^{O(1)}$, which for fixed $g$ is \emph{quasi-polynomial}, strictly weaker than the conjectured polynomial bound.

\paragraph{The genus-to-treewidth bound is false as stated.} The correct bound is $O(\sqrt{(g+1)\,n})$; the $+1$ is essential. The version $O(\sqrt{g\,n})$ collapses to $0$ at $g = 0$, yet the planar $\sqrt{n}\times\sqrt{n}$ grid has treewidth $\Theta(\sqrt{n})$.

\paragraph{Hardness backdrop.} Minimum-label cut problems are NP-hard in general; if GLMC is NP-hard on planar graphs, a polynomial-in-$n$-and-$p$ algorithm at $g=0$ would imply $P = NP$, making the conjectured polynomial bound implausible. A quasi-polynomial bound is consistent with hardness.

\paragraph{Foundational gaps.} The structural prerequisites (treewidth, tree decompositions, graph-minor/grid-minor theory, genus theory) are unavailable as formal infrastructure here, so steps (1)--(3) have no formal basis. The established, foundation-independent content is the model and theory above.

\section{Worked examples}

\begin{example}[Barbell]
Two triangles internally labeled $r$, joined by a single bridge labeled $b$. The triangle-vs-triangle split crosses only $b$ (value $1$); connectivity forbids a value-$0$ cut, so $\mathrm{glmcOpt} = 1$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Already disconnected]
Two triangles with no bridge: the split crosses no edge, so $\mathrm{glmcOpt} = 0$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Palette bound tightness]
A construction forcing every proper cut to hit all $p$ labels yields $\mathrm{glmcOpt} = p$, showing $\mathrm{glmcOpt} \le p$ is worst-case tight.
\end{example}

\section{Applications}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Communication resilience.} Labels = fiber conduits; $\mathrm{glmcOpt}$ = fewest conduit-types isolating a region.
\item \textbf{Shared infrastructure corridors.} Labels = shared corridor-types; $\mathrm{glmcOpt}$ = corridor-type fragility.
\item \textbf{Systems biology.} Labels = regulatory molecules; $\mathrm{glmcOpt}$ = fewest molecules to modularize a network.
\item \textbf{Cybersecurity.} Labels = vulnerability classes; $\mathrm{glmcOpt}$ = smallest exploit toolkit partitioning a system.
\end{itemize}

The common thread is a \emph{shared mode of failure}: when many connections fail together because they belong to one administrative, physical, chemical, or logical class, the right unit of cost is the class, and GLMC is its canonical optimization.

\section{Limitations and threats to validity}

Three caveats deserve emphasis. (i) \emph{Computational scope.} The verified solver is exponential; nothing here yields a practical large-$n$ algorithm, and Section~6 argues none should be expected in general. (ii) \emph{Modeling faithfulness.} The directed-triple encoding of undirected edges is faithful for every quantity defined (each depends only on the unordered endpoint pair), but a user should confirm their application counts \emph{distinct labels} rather than weighted or multiplicity-sensitive costs. (iii) \emph{Hardness is cited, not proved here.} The NP-hardness backdrop of Section~6 is standard for minimum-label cut problems and used only as a plausibility caution; we do not formalize a reduction.

\section{Discussion and future work}

We have established a trustworthy kernel: a faithful finite model, the palette upper bound, existence of proper cuts, two-sided correctness of the optimum, the zero-optimum disconnection criterion, and an exact brute-force solver certified against the definition. We also delineate the negative space: the conjectured fast surface algorithm fails as stated, and a polynomial general algorithm is implausible under standard assumptions.

Future directions: (i) submodularity/matroid analysis of the component-count function on label sets; (ii) recursive label-bucketing toward sub-brute-force exact algorithms verifiable against BF; (iii) a labeled Menger-type min-cut/max-flow duality; and (iv) parameterized-complexity classification (FPT in the optimum value $k$ vs.\ W[1]-hardness in $p$). The verified baseline is what makes these conjectures sharply falsifiable.

\section{Conclusion}

The value of this work lies less in any single theorem than in the completeness and reliability of the package as a whole. We isolated the well-posed core of GLMC and proved, end to end, the statements a downstream user needs: that the objective is a finite, well-defined natural number; that it never exceeds the palette size $p$; that a proper cut exists whenever the graph is nontrivial; that the optimum is simultaneously a lower bound on every cut and an attained minimum, so brute-force enumeration is correct; and that a value of zero is exactly the fingerprint of an already-separated graph. Each is elementary by design: a foundation should be unsurprising, exhaustively checkable, and free of hidden assumptions. Equally deliberate is the candor about the runtime conjecture: rather than advertise an unobtainable fixed-genus polynomial algorithm, we record precisely why the proposed pipeline fails, steering future effort toward the genuinely open questions and away from a shortcut that does not exist. The result is a small, sturdy, and honest platform on which sharper algorithmic theory can be built.

